# Buffer Overflows Explained

A buffer overflow occurs when you write more data to a buffer than it can hold, causing data to spill into adjacent memory locations.

## How They Happen

```c
// UNSAFE CODE - DO NOT USE
void vulnerable_function() {
    char buffer[8];
    strcpy(buffer, "This string is way too long!");  // Overflow!
}
```

**Memory layout:**
```
[buffer: 8 bytes][return address][other data]
                 ↑ Gets overwritten!
```

## Common Causes

### 1. **Unsafe String Functions**
```c
// DANGEROUS
strcpy(dest, src);      // No bounds checking
strcat(dest, src);      // No bounds checking
gets(buffer);           // Never use this!
sprintf(buffer, ...);   // No size limit
```

### 2. **Unchecked Array Access**
```c
int arr[10];
arr[15] = 42;  // Out of bounds write
```

## Safe Alternatives

### Use Bounded Functions
```c
// SAFE
char buffer[64];

// Instead of strcpy:
strncpy(buffer, source, sizeof(buffer) - 1);
buffer[sizeof(buffer) - 1] = '\0';  // Ensure null termination

// Better: use snprintf
snprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), "%s", source);

// Instead of gets:
fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), stdin);

// Instead of strcat:
strncat(buffer, source, sizeof(buffer) - strlen(buffer) - 1);
```

### Check Array Bounds
```c
size_t index = get_index();
if (index < array_size) {
    array[index] = value;  // Safe
}
```

### Modern C Functions (C11)
```c
// Bounds-checking interfaces (if available)
strcpy_s(dest, dest_size, src);
strcat_s(dest, dest_size, src);
```

## Best Practices

1. **Always know your buffer size**
2. **Validate input lengths before copying**
3. **Use `sizeof()` instead of hardcoded sizes**
4. **Enable compiler warnings** (`-Wall -Wextra`)
5. **Use static analysis tools** (Valgrind, AddressSanitizer)
6. **Consider using safer languages** for security-critical code

## Example: Safe Input Handling

```c
#define MAX_INPUT 256

void safe_input() {
    char buffer[MAX_INPUT];

    printf("Enter name: ");
    if (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), stdin) != NULL) {
        // Remove trailing newline
        size_t len = strlen(buffer);
        if (len > 0 && buffer[len-1] == '\n') {
            buffer[len-1] = '\0';
        }

        printf("Hello, %s!\n", buffer);
    }
}
```

Buffer overflows are the root cause of many security vulnerabilities. Always validate sizes!

# Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Explained

## How XSS Works

XSS attacks occur when an attacker injects malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. The browser executes this code because it trusts content from your server.

### Basic Attack Example

```html
<!-- Vulnerable code -->
<div>Welcome, <?php echo $_GET['name']; ?></div>

<!-- Attacker uses: ?name=<script>alert('XSS')</script> -->
<!-- Result: -->
<div>Welcome, <script>alert('XSS')</script></div>
```

## Three Types of XSS

### 1. **Reflected XSS** (Non-Persistent)
Malicious script comes from the current HTTP request.

```javascript
// Vulnerable search feature
app.get('/search', (req, res) => {
  res.send(`You searched for: ${req.query.q}`);
});

// Attack: /search?q=<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>
```

### 2. **Stored XSS** (Persistent)
Malicious script is saved in your database.

```javascript
// Vulnerable comment system
app.post('/comment', (req, res) => {
  db.save(req.body.comment); // Stores: <script>steal_cookies()</script>
});

// Later, shown to all users:
app.get('/comments', (req, res) => {
  res.send(db.getAllComments()); // Executes malicious script
});
```

### 3. **DOM-Based XSS**
Vulnerability exists in client-side code.

```javascript
// Vulnerable JavaScript
const name = window.location.hash.substring(1);
document.getElementById('welcome').innerHTML = name;

// Attack: page.html#<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>
```

## Proper Input Sanitization

### 1. **Output Encoding** (Primary Defense)

```javascript
// HTML Context
function escapeHtml(unsafe) {
  return unsafe
    .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
    .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
    .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
    .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
    .replace(/'/g, "&#039;");
}

// Usage
res.send(`<div>Welcome, ${escapeHtml(userInput)}</div>`);
```

### 2. **Context-Specific Encoding**

```javascript
// JavaScript Context
function escapeJs(unsafe) {
  return unsafe
    .replace(/\\/g, '\\\\')
    .replace(/'/g, "\\'")
    .replace(/"/g, '\\"')
    .replace(/\n/g, '\\n')
    .replace(/\r/g, '\\r');
}

// URL Context
const safe = encodeURIComponent(userInput);

// CSS Context - avoid user input in CSS or use strict validation
```

### 3. **Use Template Engines with Auto-Escaping**

```javascript
// React (auto-escapes by default)
function Welcome({ name }) {
  return <div>Welcome, {name}</div>; // Safe
}

// Handlebars
<div>Welcome, {{name}}</div> <!-- Auto-escaped -->
<div>Welcome, {{{name}}}</div> <!-- NOT escaped - dangerous -->

// EJS
<div>Welcome, <%= name %></div> <!-- Escaped -->
<div>Welcome, <%- name %></div> <!-- NOT escaped - dangerous -->
```

### 4. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**

```javascript
// Express middleware
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.setHeader(
    "Content-Security-Policy",
    "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; object-src 'none';"
  );
  next();
});
```

### 5. **Input Validation** (Secondary Defense)

```javascript
