# Centering a Div in CSS

Here are the most common and reliable methods:

## Method 1: Flexbox (Recommended)

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  height: 100vh;            /* parent needs height */
}
```

```html
<div class="parent">
  <div class="child">I'm centered!</div>
</div>
```

## Method 2: Grid (Simplest)

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
}
```

## Method 3: Absolute Positioning + Transform

Useful when you can't change the parent's display type.

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

## Method 4: Margin Auto (Fixed-size elements)

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  margin: auto;
  width: 200px;  /* must have defined width/height */
  height: 100px;
}
```

---

## Which should you use?

| Method | Best For |
|---|---|
| **Flexbox** | Most modern use cases, flexible content |
| **Grid** | Simplest syntax, single item centering |
| **Absolute + Transform** | When parent layout can't change |
| **Margin Auto** | Legacy support, fixed dimensions |

**My recommendation:** Use **Flexbox** or **Grid** for new projects—they're well-supported, easy to read, and handle dynamic content sizes gracefully.
